// ---- Passively scan a branch (burpsy-react-ui-p09) ---------------------------------------------
//
// Burp offers "Passively scan this branch" and "Passively scan selected branches" from the site map.
// burpsy had the scanner and no way to point it at a subtree.
//
// # The two decisions this needed before it needed code
//
// **What risk class.** A passive sweep sends nothing: it re-reads responses already in the store. So
// it fits none of the manifest's classes by analogy, and copying /api/scanner/audit's riskAttack
// would have been wrong. riskRead understates it — a sweep produces findings, and findings are what
// an operator reports, so manufacturing them under an unattended caller's control is not a read.
// riskAttack overstates it and would demand confirmation for an operation that touches no target,
// which trains operators to click through the confirmations that matter. It is riskLocal: it mutates
// burpsy's own findings state and sends no traffic. Recorded in the manifest next to the route, which
// is the whole reason that field exists.
//
// **What scope means for bytes already captured.** This is the one with a wrong answer that looks
// right. The responses were captured earlier, possibly while the scope was wider than it is now, so
// re-scanning can produce a finding about a host the operator has since excluded.
//
// The choice made here: the sweep FILTERS by current scope, and reports how many exchanges it skipped
// for that reason. Scope is the standing statement of what this engagement may touch, and a finding
// about an excluded host is one an operator cannot act on and should not report. But skipping
// silently would make the counts unreconcilable against the branch — an operator would point at 40
// paths, see 12 scanned, and have no way to learn why. So the count is partitioned and the skip has
// its own field.
//
// # Why the counts are a partition and not a summary
//
// scanned + skipped_out_of_scope + skipped_no_response == matched, always. internal/crawler's Report
// is the model: a caller can always answer "what happened to the other ones", which is what makes a
// report readable as coverage rather than as a highlight reel. A sweep that answered only
// "12 findings" could not be reconciled against anything the operator can see.

// passiveBranchReq names the subtree to sweep.
type passiveBranchReq struct {
	// Host is required. A sweep with no host is the whole store, which is what the live audit already
	// does and is not what "this branch" means.
	Host string `json:"host"`
	// PathPrefix narrows within the host. Empty means the whole host, which is Burp's "scan this
	// branch" pointed at the root.
	PathPrefix string `json:"path_prefix"`
	// Port, when non-zero, narrows to one port. Zero means every port on the host, because a site map
	// branch is an authority and an operator pointing at example.com means both 80 and 443.
	Port uint16 `json:"port"`
	// Exclude is Burp's pre-scan filter step (burpsy-react-ui-a13), as a query over this package's own
	// filter grammar: exchanges the query does not match are dropped from the sweep before the
	// scanner sees them. Empty means no filter, so every caller predating this field is unchanged.
	//
	// A query that MATCHES the items to drop — the opposite polarity from /api/history?q=, which
	// keeps what it matches. Same grammar, same adapter, opposite question, and the field is named
	// `exclude` so the polarity is stated rather than inferred.
	//
	// A query rather than four booleans because the filters and the filter language are then one
	// thing. Burp's shipped list (`extension != "js"` and its siblings) is a query like any other, an
	// operator can narrow past the presets, and "what filtered this sweep" is a string the audit
	// trail can record — none of which four booleans would give.
	Exclude string `json:"exclude,omitempty"`
}

// passiveBranchResp is the sweep's whole outcome.
type passiveBranchResp struct {
	Host       string `json:"host"`
	PathPrefix string `json:"path_prefix,omitempty"`
	Port       uint16 `json:"port,omitempty"`

	// Matched is every stored exchange in the branch, before any filtering.
	Matched int `json:"matched"`
	// Scanned is how many were handed to the scanner.
	Scanned int `json:"scanned"`
	// SkippedOutOfScope is how many were in the branch and refused by the CURRENT scope. Its own
	// field because these were captured — possibly under a wider scope — and an operator who cannot
	// see this number cannot reconcile Scanned against Matched.
	SkippedOutOfScope int `json:"skipped_out_of_scope"`
	// SkippedNoResponse is how many had no response to scan. A passive check reads a response; a
	// request still in flight is not a failure and not a clean result either.
	SkippedNoResponse int `json:"skipped_no_response"`

	// Findings is how many the sweep produced, after the finding store's own deduplication — so it
	// is what an operator will actually see, not how many times a check fired.
	Findings int `json:"findings"`
	// Filtered is how many were in the branch and removed by the operator's pre-scan filter, before
	// any of the sweep's own gates. Its own field for the reason the others have one: without it,
	// "12 scanned of 40" cannot be reconciled — an operator who asked to drop static assets and then
	// watched 20 of them vanish would have no way to learn the filter ran at all.
	Filtered int `json:"filtered"`
	// Exclude echoes the filter the sweep actually applied, so the response states the rule rather
	// than leaving the caller to remember what it sent.
	Exclude string `json:"exclude,omitempty"`
	// Loaded is how many passive checks were available. Zero means the sweep ran and could not have
	// found anything, which is not the same as a clean branch, and the caller must not render them
	// alike.
	Loaded int `json:"loaded"`
	// Summary states the counts with their caveats in one line, so a surface that renders nothing
	// else still cannot present a partial result as a complete one.
	Summary string `json:"summary"`
}

// handlePassiveBranch serves POST /api/scanner/passive-branch.
func (s *Server) handlePassiveBranch(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req passiveBranchReq
	if err := requestJSON(w, r, nil).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "Invalid request body", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	req.Host = strings.TrimSpace(req.Host)
	if req.Host == "" {
		http.Error(w, "A host is required: a sweep with no host is the whole store, which is what the "+
			"live audit already does and is not what scanning a branch means", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	if s.passiveScanner == nil {
		http.Error(w, "No passive scanner is attached to this server, so nothing could be scanned",
			http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
		return
	}

	// The pre-scan filter (a13), compiled once and refused with the grammar's own error — the caret
	// included — rather than a boolean that silently does nothing. An operator who typed a filter the
	// server ignored would be reading a sweep that did not honour it, which is the exact failure
	// the honest-counting rule exists to prevent.
	//
	// Empty means no filter rather than a query that matches nothing: a nil *queryFilter matches
	// everything, which is what an absent filter has to mean, and the same shape /api/history?q= uses.
	var filter *queryFilter
	if trimmed := strings.TrimSpace(req.Exclude); trimmed != "" {
		var err error
		filter, err = compileQuery(trimmed, s.store)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, "The pre-scan filter is not a valid query:\n"+query.Render(err), http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
	}

	resp := passiveBranchResp{
		Host:       req.Host,
		PathPrefix: req.PathPrefix,
		Port:       req.Port,
		Exclude:    strings.TrimSpace(req.Exclude),
		Loaded:     s.passiveScanner.Loaded(),
	}

	// The task exists so the sweep appears on the dashboard like every other unit of work, and so its
	// findings carry a task id an operator can correlate. Total is unknown until the store has been
	// walked, so it starts at 0 and the breakdown at the end carries the real numbers.
	task := s.tasks.Start(dash.KindPassiveBranch, req.Host+req.PathPrefix, 0)

	for _, ex := range s.store.Exchanges() {
		if !branchContains(ex, req) {
			continue
		}
		resp.Matched++

		// The operator's filter, ahead of every gate the sweep owns: a filtered exchange is one the
		// operator asked not to spend on, so it leaves before the no-response and scope partitions
		// count it. Putting it after them would report the same exchange as both filtered and
		// skipped, and the partition would no longer add up.
		if filter != nil && filter.match(ex) {
			resp.Filtered++
			continue
		}

		if ex.Response == nil {
			// A passive check reads a response. No response is not a clean result.
			resp.SkippedNoResponse++
			continue
		}
		// Current scope, not the scope in force when this was captured. See the file comment: a
		// finding about a host the operator has since excluded is one they cannot act on.
		if !s.scope.Contains(ex.Host, ex.Port, ex.Path, ex.TLS) {
			resp.SkippedOutOfScope++
			continue
		}

		resp.Scanned++
		for _, f := range s.passiveScanner.Scan(ex) {
			s.findings.Add(s.activeFinding(f, task.ID()))
			resp.Findings++
		}
	}

	resp.Summary = passiveBranchSummary(resp)

	// The breakdown is the same partition the response carries, in the dashboard's vocabulary, so the
	// coverage summary cannot disagree with what this endpoint answered. Gated, not Skipped, for the
	// scope refusals: those are work a policy declined, which is what Gated means here, and folding
	// them into Skipped would lose the reason.

	PauseOnTimeoutPercent *int `json:"pause_task_requests_timed_out_percentage,omitempty"`
	PauseOnAuthFailures   *int `json:"consecutive_authentication_failures_before_pausing_task,omitempty"`
	FollowUpPasses        *int `json:"number_of_follow_up_passes,omitempty"`
	// Pool names a saved resource pool to run under. Empty leaves the crawl on this package's own
	// documented defaults, which is what every caller predating pools sends.
	//
	// It is checked against the saved set and an unknown name is a 400 — never a fallback to the
	// defaults. A crawl that ran at five requests a second because its pool had been renamed, while
	// the operator believed a two-a-second pool was in force, is precisely the failure a named pool
	// exists to prevent.
	Pool string `json:"pool"`
	// RequestsPerSecond is the inline form, kept working: a pool is a resolution step, not a
	// replacement. Naming both is refused rather than silently resolved one way — see handleCrawlStart.
	RequestsPerSecond float64           `json:"requests_per_second"`
	UserAgent         string            `json:"user_agent"`
	Headers           map[string]string `json:"headers"`
}

// validateCrawlBounds refuses a bound that is not a bound.
//
// REFUSED rather than clamped, and the distinction is the point. A negative limit is not "no limit"
// and not "the default" — it is a request that cannot be honoured, and the three answers available
// to the engine (treat it as the default, treat it as zero, treat it as unlimited) each produce a
// crawl that reports a result nobody asked for. Clamping to the default would be the worst of the
// three: the crawl runs, fills its frontier, stops on the default, and the report names a stop the
// operator never chose.
//
// Zero is ACCEPTED, which is why the eight fields are pointers. Zero is a real answer for every
// bound in this set — zero locations, zero links per page, zero re-logins — and the only way to tell
// it from "not set" is the pointer. This is the iueb shape the audit path's OptUint32 already
// refuses to flatten, applied to the crawler.
func validateCrawlBounds(req crawlStartReq) error {
	// name/value pairs, in the order the request declares them, so a request naming several bad
	// bounds is answered with all of them rather than one per attempt.
	bad := make([]string, 0, 4)
	for _, b := range []struct {
		name  string
		value *int
	}{
		{"max_requests", intPtr(req.MaxRequests)}, {"max_depth", intPtr(req.MaxDepth)},
		{"max_locations", req.MaxLocations}, {"max_frontier", req.MaxFrontier},
		{"max_links_per_page", req.MaxLinksPerPage}, {"max_parse_bytes", req.MaxParseBytes},
		{"max_template_visits", req.MaxTemplateVisits}, {"max_capture_bytes", req.MaxCaptureBytes},
		{"max_fingerprints", req.MaxFingerprints}, {"max_relogins", req.MaxRelogins},
	} {
		if b.value != nil && *b.value < 0 {
			bad = append(bad, fmt.Sprintf("%s: %d is negative; a bound is a count of what may happen "+
				"and a negative one cannot be honoured — send 0 for none, or omit it for the default", b.name, *b.value))
		}
	}
	if req.MaxDurationSecs < 0 {
		bad = append(bad, fmt.Sprintf("max_duration_secs: %d is negative; a negative duration is not "+
			"a shorter crawl", req.MaxDurationSecs))
	}
	if len(bad) == 0 {
		return nil
	}
	return errors.New(strings.Join(bad, "; "))
}

// intPtr lifts a plain int field to the pointer shape the eight optional bounds use, so one loop
// validates both. Nil for the three legacy fields when they are zero, which is exactly their
// "unset" reading and keeps them out of the negative check.
func intPtr(v int) *int {
	if v == 0 {
		return nil
	}
	return &v
}

// crawlLimitsView is the effective bound set as the wire spells it: snake_case, seconds for the
// duration, and every field present.
//
// Present rather than omitempty, so a run record names every bound that bounded it instead of the
// console having to know which ones it may assume. MaxDuration is seconds because that is what the
// launch request takes (max_duration_secs) and what an operator reads; the engine's time.Duration is
// nanoseconds, and publishing those would be a number nobody asked for.
type crawlLimitsView struct {
	MaxRequests       int `json:"max_requests"`
	MaxDepth          int `json:"max_depth"`
	MaxDurationSecs   int `json:"max_duration_secs"`
	MaxLocations      int `json:"max_locations"`
	MaxFrontier       int `json:"max_frontier"`
	MaxLinksPerPage   int `json:"max_links_per_page"`
	MaxParseBytes     int `json:"max_parse_bytes"`
	MaxTemplateVisits int `json:"max_template_visits"`
	MaxCaptureBytes   int `json:"max_capture_bytes"`
	MaxFingerprints   int `json:"max_fingerprints"`
	MaxRelogins       int `json:"max_relogins"`
}

func newCrawlLimitsView(l crawler.Limits) *crawlLimitsView {
	return &crawlLimitsView{
		MaxRequests:       l.MaxRequests,
		MaxDepth:          l.MaxDepth,
		MaxDurationSecs:   int(l.MaxDuration / time.Second),
		MaxLocations:      l.MaxLocations,
		MaxFrontier:       l.MaxFrontier,
		MaxLinksPerPage:   l.MaxLinksPerPage,
		MaxParseBytes:     l.MaxParseBytes,
		MaxTemplateVisits: l.MaxTemplateVisits,
		MaxCaptureBytes:   l.MaxCaptureBytes,
		MaxFingerprints:   l.MaxFingerprints,
		MaxRelogins:       l.MaxRelogins,
	}
}

// handleCrawl serves POST /api/crawl (start) and GET /api/crawl (list, or one by ?task=).
func (s *Server) handleCrawl(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	switch r.Method {
	case http.MethodGet:
		s.handleCrawlGet(w, r)
	case http.MethodPost:
		s.handleCrawlStart(w, r)
	default:
		http.Error(w, "Method not allowed", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
	}
}

func (s *Server) handleCrawlGet(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	if raw := r.URL.Query().Get("task"); raw != "" {
		id, err := strconv.ParseUint(raw, 10, 64)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, "task must be a whole number", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		run, ok := s.crawls.get(id)
		if !ok {
			http.Error(w, fmt.Sprintf("No crawl %d is retained", id), http.StatusNotFound)
			return
		}
		writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, run)
		return
	}
	writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]any{"crawls": s.crawls.list(), "default_limits": CrawlDefaults})
}

func (s *Server) handleCrawlStart(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req crawlStartReq
	if err := requestJSON(w, r, http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, maxRequestBodyBytes)).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "Invalid JSON body", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	taskID, seeds, liveTaken, unapplied, err := s.startCrawl(req)
	if err != nil {
		http.Error(w, err.Error(), crawlStartStatus(err))
		return
	}
	run, ok := s.crawls.get(taskID)
	state, kind := "", crawlKindCrawl
	if ok {
		state = run.State
		kind = run.Kind
	}
	writeJSON(w, http.StatusAccepted, map[string]any{
		"task_id": taskID,
		"seeds":   seeds,
		// The live-seen count the seeds include (tqug): an operator who asked for live capture
		// needs to know how much of the seed list came from the wire rather than their typing.
		"live_seen_used": liveTaken,
		"state":          state,
		// Echoed for the same reason /api/scanner/audit echoes config_name: a reply that looked the
		// same whether or not the audit half was accepted could not be told apart from one that
		// accepted the kind and ran a plain crawl.
		"kind": kind,
		// The keys the named configuration set that this engine did not apply, the same
		// not-silent half the audit path reports (burpsy-react-ui-3ok6). Present because the crawler
		// object in a shipped Burp configuration was previously parsed into nothing at all: an
		// operator importing one had no way to know a third of it had not arrived.
		"config_name":           req.Config,
		"config_unapplied_keys": unapplied,
		// Seeds the engagement scope refuses (us6r.3): the crawl starts from the others, and these are
		// named so the console can say so rather than report them as nothing.
		"out_of_scope_seeds": s.outOfScopeSeeds(seeds),
	})
}

// crawlStartStatus maps a start failure from any scan launch to its status. A pool the operator named
// and that is not saved is 404 — the request is well formed and the world is not what they thought —
// while a malformed configuration is 400. Told apart because the fix differs: reload versus retype.
func crawlStartStatus(err error) int {
	if errors.Is(err, errSeedsOutOfScope) {
		return http.StatusForbidden
	}
	if errors.Is(err, pool.ErrNoSuchPool) {
		return http.StatusNotFound
	}
	return http.StatusBadRequest
}

// resolveRunRate turns the two ways a caller can state a scan's rate — a saved pool's name, or an
// inline requests-per-second — into the resolved limit a run gets, or nil for this package's defaults.
//
// Shared by every launch surface rather than repeated in each, because the two rules it encodes are
// the same two for all of them: naming both is a refusal rather than an order of precedence (a scan
// that ran at a rate the operator typed in a box they were told was ignored has no way to find out),
// and a named pool that is not saved is an error rather than a silent fall back to the defaults.
func (s *Server) resolveRunRate(poolName string, requestsPerSecond float64) (*intruder.RateLimit, error) {
	if poolName == "" {
		return nil, nil
	}
	if requestsPerSecond > 0 {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf(
			"this scan names both the pool %q and requests_per_second %v; the pool already states a "+
				"rate, so send one or the other", poolName, requestsPerSecond)
	}
	limit, err := s.pools.Limit(poolName)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%w (saved pools: %s)", err, availablePools(s.pools))
	}
	return &limit, nil
}

// startCrawl validates a crawl request, registers the task and starts the crawl, returning the task
// id and the seeds it actually took.
//
// Split out of the handler so a *scheduled* crawl is the same crawl: the schedule runner hands its
// saved params here rather than re-implementing seed trimming, limit defaults and pool resolution.
// Two code paths that both "start a crawl" would drift, and the one that drifted would be the
// unattended one nobody watches.
// scanKind normalises the requested scan kind, refusing anything it does not recognise.
//
// Empty means the plain crawl, because that is what every caller predating the field asked for by
// omission. Anything else non-empty must name one of the two constants: an unknown kind is refused
// with the accepted set in the message, never quietly downgraded to a crawl.
func scanKind(requested string) (string, error) {
	switch requested {
	case "", crawlKindCrawl:
		return crawlKindCrawl, nil
	case crawlKindAudit:
		return crawlKindAudit, nil
	}
	return "", fmt.Errorf(
		"unknown scan kind %q; this build runs %q and %q", requested, crawlKindCrawl, crawlKindAudit)
}

// crawlRenderer is the browser hand-off the crawler's rendered-DOM pass runs on
// (burpsy-react-ui-nx7b): the SAME lazy provider the audit's DOM checks use, so one process
// runs one browser rather than one per subsystem, and a crawl started with no -dom gets a
// nil function — which is the previous crawl to the byte, because a nil Render means no
// rendered pass and no skip recorded (nothing was declined).
//
// Built per crawl rather than stored on the server, because the provider is the scanner's
// capability, not the web layer's: the audit scanner is constructed with its browser at
// startup (cmd/burpsy/main.go), and the crawl borrows it for exactly as long as the crawl
// runs. A scan whose DOM checks declined for a missing browser reports the same decline to
// the crawl, which records it as SkipRenderUnavailable per URL rather than pretending the
// wire parse was the whole story.
func (s *Server) crawlRenderer(scope crawler.Scope) func(ctx context.Context, url string) (string, crawler.RenderOutcome, error) {
	return s.crawlRendererIn(nil, scope, nil)
}

// crawlRendererIn is crawlRenderer inside a recorded login's session (burpsy-react-ui-xhtk), so an
// authenticated crawl's rendered pass sees the logged-in application rather than its login page. A
// nil session is the ordinary renderer.
//
// Without a session every render runs in a fresh scope-gated browser context whose gate is the CRAWL's
// scope (burpsy-react-ui-browser-render-unscoped) — the crawlInteractor's arrangement. The page was
// admitted by the crawl, but the scripts, beacons, XHR and frames it pulls in while it renders were
// not, and an ungated render sent them wherever the page pointed. Refusals reach the crawl as a
// per-page skip record (RenderOutcome.Blocked), so a page rendered without its out-of-scope CDN reads

